# PHP / Laravel patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to idiomatic
Laravel application design and its common anti-patterns. Applies only to
`*.php` files.

## Layered responsibility

- Controllers orchestrate; they do not contain business rules. Push
  non-trivial logic into an Eloquent model method, an invokable action
  class (`app/Actions/...`), or a service class injected through the
  constructor.
- A Form Request class owns validation rules and authorization for one
  request shape (`rules()`, `authorize()`); do not duplicate the same
  validation array across multiple controller methods when a shared Form
  Request would do.
- Eloquent model classes own their own data shape (casts, relationships,
  scopes, accessors); keep cross-model orchestration (a multi-model
  transaction, a workflow spanning several models) in a service or action
  class, not stuffed into one model's methods.

## Eloquent relationships and queries

- Eager-load a relationship (`with()`, or `load()` after the fact) before
  iterating a collection and accessing that relationship inside the loop;
  a relationship accessed per-row with no eager load is an N+1 query, not
  "just how Eloquent works."
- For a result set too large to hold in memory, use `chunk()`/`chunkById()`
  or `cursor()`/`lazy()` instead of `all()`/an unbounded `get()`;
  `chunkById()` (not plain `chunk()`) when the loop body updates or
  deletes rows, since plain `chunk()`'s offset pagination skips rows as
  the underlying set shifts.
- Use query scopes (`scopeActive`, `scopePublished`) for a filter reused
  across more than one call site instead of repeating the same `where()`
  chain.

## Dependency injection and the service container

- Type-hint a constructor/method dependency and let the container resolve
  it instead of calling `app(SomeClass::class)`/`resolve(...)` inline
  inside business logic — reserve container-pulling for genuinely
  dynamic resolution (a class name only known at runtime).
- Bind an interface to its implementation in a service provider's
  `register()` when a class needs to be swappable (a payment gateway, a
  notification channel); do not introduce an interface with exactly one
  implementation and no swapping need "for testability" alone when
  Laravel's own container-based mocking (`$this->mock(...)` in tests)
  already covers that.

## Jobs, events, and idempotency

- A queued job that can be delivered more than once (Laravel's queues are
  at-least-once) either implements `ShouldBeUnique`/`uniqueId()`, or
  checks/records its own side effect before repeating it — do not assume
  a job runs exactly once.
- Prefer a domain event (`event(new OrderShipped($order))`) with
  dedicated listeners over stacking unrelated side effects directly in
  the code path that triggers them, when more than one unrelated concern
  needs to react to the same occurrence.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A "fat controller" that validates, queries, transforms, and formats the
  response all inline — split by responsibility as above.
- A model with `protected $guarded = []` used outside of a factory/seeder
  context — this disables mass-assignment protection entirely and should
  be treated as a defect, not a convenience default.
- Business logic duplicated between a web controller and an API
  controller for the same underlying action — extract the shared action/
  service both call.
- A trait used to share mutable state between unrelated models instead of
  genuinely shared, stateless behavior — prefer composition (an injected
  collaborator) when the "shared behavior" is really shared state.
